{
  "about": "Euphonia in the languages of the cultures the book names, for the ribbons. Each word is printed in the dictionary cited, with the sense quoted from its entry; none is composed or translated here. Where a language's dictionaries give several, the word nearest harmony is taken (harmony or concord, then euphony, then melody, then a sweet voice).",
  "spelling": "As printed, but for marks a dictionary adds only as a reading aid: Latin vowel lengths, Malay's breve, Italian's stress accent, compound hyphens (Icelandic, Xhosa's noun prefix), Harkavy's double hyphen for the Yiddish maqaf and the middle dot of Carter's Sinhala. Tone and stress marks that belong to the spelling (Twi, Kikongo, Malagasy) are kept. Jäschke prints sgra-dbyaṅs only in his romanisation; it is set here in Tibetan script, letter for letter.",
